.TH EVENT_LOOP_SHUTDOWN 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_shutdown \- stop an event loop's reactor thread(s)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t event_loop_shutdown(event_loop loop);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Signals every one of loop's background reactor threads to stop and
blocks until all of them have been joined.  No dispatch can be in
flight once this call returns.

Idempotent: safe to call more than once, including concurrently from
multiple threads.  Exactly one caller actually performs the joins;
every other concurrent caller blocks until those joins complete before
returning, so every caller's own "blocks until joined" contract holds
regardless of which one triggered the shutdown.

event_loop_destroy() calls this internally if the caller never called it
explicitly, so calling it directly first is optional; do so when the
"reactor threads stopped" and "resources freed" steps need to happen at
different times.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  event_loop to shut down.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        Shut down (or already was).
ccol_invalid_args   loop is NULL.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call concurrently from multiple threads other than one of loop's
own reactor threads.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Must not be called from within a callback running on one of loop's own
reactor threads.  This function joins every reactor thread internally,
including the one running the callback that (indirectly) called it; a
thread cannot join itself (undefined behavior / EDEADLK).  Defer
shutdown to another thread, or to after the callback returns, instead.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR event_loop_create (3)
